# zpwi-csid scope and references

## Search scope

Limit all codebase searches, file exploration, and grep to `/home/pwi/src/zpwi-csid` and its subdirectories. Do not search sibling repos or parent paths unless the user explicitly asks.

## Project context

This project is for CSID (Congenital Sucrase-Isomaltase Deficiency): tracking and evaluating good vs bad foods for people managing sucrose, maltose, and starch tolerance.

## Default reference

**Australia (Phase 2):** default to [English — What Can I Eat With Sucrase-Isomaltase Deficiency in Australia](https://www.drcarolynenglish.com/post/what-can-i-eat-with-sucrase-isomaltase-deficiency-in-australia) and Australian composition (AUSNUT 2023 framework; sucrose/maltose/starch from [FSANZ AFCD](https://www.foodstandards.gov.au/science-data/food-nutrient-databases/afcd/data-files)). Local copy of the English article may exist at `uploads/what-can-i-eat-with-sucrase-isomaltase-deficiency-in-australia-0.md`.

English traffic lights (per 100 g, sucrose and starch independently): low ≤1 g, moderate >1–≤2 g, higher >2 g. Foods with **≤1 g sucrose AND ≤1 g starch per 100 g** are treated as allow unless the user says otherwise. The published AUSNUT 2023 survey nutrient file reports starch and total sugars, not sucrose — use AFCD Release 3 (or the Bond SSRD dataset) for sucrose.

**Portion load (required — English garlic principle):** Per-100 g traffic lights compare foods; they are not always the eating amount. After AFCD values, estimate sucrose and starch at the **quantity typically used** (clove, cube, tsp, handful, recipe share). If that load is **≤1 g sucrose AND ≤1 g starch**, treat as allow and record the defined portion in `notes` (same pattern as garlic / stock cubes). If per 100 g is above the low band but English Table 3–5 gives a practical ~1 g portion (many fruits, veg, nuts), keep status avoid-or-portion-test and put the calculated amount in `notes`. Do **not** classify concentrates, cubes, powders, pastes, or seasonings as bad solely because an ingredient list shows sugar or maltodextrin, or solely from dry per-100 g values, without quantifying the serve.

**Step 1 elimination baseline:** [Ideal Nutrition CSID guide](https://www.idealnutrition.com.au/csid-how-to-manage-sucrase-isomaltase-deficiency/). Local copy may exist at `uploads/csid-how-to-manage-sucrase-isomaltase-deficiency-0.md`.

For US/overseas tiered tolerance lists (most / some / few CSID patients), also use [CSID Cares — Choosing Your Foods](https://www.csidcares.org/treatment/diet/). Local copy may exist at `uploads/diet-0.md`.

## Researching food classifications

For Australian foods and Phase 2, use **English / AUSNUT / AFCD as primary**. Use Ideal Nutrition for Step 1 elimination history and when AU data are missing. When a food is missing, ambiguous, or conflicts, consult `meta.research_sources` in [data/foods.yaml](../data/foods.yaml) and cross-check before classifying. Note cultivar conflicts (e.g. yellow-skin vs white-skin honeydew; Kent/Jarrahdale vs butternut pumpkin).

| Source | Best for |
| --- | --- |
| [English — SID in Australia](https://www.drcarolynenglish.com/post/what-can-i-eat-with-sucrase-isomaltase-deficiency-in-australia) | Australian primary; AUSNUT 2023 traffic lights; Kent/Jarrahdale pumpkin; yellow-skin honeydew; 1 g sucrose portions |
| [SSRD Dataset Australia](https://research.bond.edu.au/en/datasets/ssrd-dataset-australia/) | Full English et al. AUSNUT-derived spreadsheet |
| [FSANZ AFCD Release 3](https://www.foodstandards.gov.au/science-data/food-nutrient-databases/afcd/data-files) | Sucrose, maltose, starch g/100 g for named AU foods |
| [Ideal Nutrition CSID guide](https://www.idealnutrition.com.au/csid-how-to-manage-sucrase-isomaltase-deficiency/) | Strict elimination baseline; reintroduction steps |
| [CSID Cares — Choosing Your Foods](https://www.csidcares.org/treatment/diet/) | Tiered fruit/vegetable/dairy tolerance; food diary; composition tables |
| [ANTHC CSID Store-Bought Food Guide (PDF)](https://anthc.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/CSID-Store-Bought-Food-Guide.pdf) | Usually vs least tolerated; label checks |
| [ANTHC CSID Educational Booklet (PDF)](https://anthc.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/CSID-Educational-Booklet.pdf) | Sucrose/starch overview; tiered tolerance |
| [CSID Made Simple — food list](https://csidmadesimple.com/csid-food-list/) | Vegetables/dairy by tolerance tier (most / some / few) |
| [CSIDinfo.com dairy table](http://www.csidinfo.com/dairy.htm) | Grams of sucrose, starch, lactose per dairy serving |
| [Leeds NHS — CSID dietary advice](https://www.leedsth.nhs.uk/patients/resources/dietary-advice-for-congenital-sucrose-isomaltase-deficiency/) | Clinical NHS guidance; starch substitutes |
| [Jenna Volpe RDN — low sucrose list](https://wholeisticliving.com/2023/01/16/low-sucrose-foods-list/) | Dietitian low-sucrose vegetable list |

**Workflow:** (1) For Australian foods / Phase 2, check English then AFCD sucrose+starch (both, independently). (2) Apply the portion-load step above for any food not eaten as ~100 g (concentrates, seasonings, nuts/seeds, Table 3–5 foods). (3) Compare Ideal Nutrition Step 1 if the food was an elimination avoid. (4) If still unclear, search the overseas sources above. (5) Record `sources` on the food entry when classification relies on English/AFCD or other non-baseline references. (6) Note cultivar and country conflicts (e.g. yellow-skin vs white-skin honeydew; Kent/Jarrahdale vs butternut pumpkin). (7) Do not assume good/bad from labels alone or without a cited source or explicit user instruction.
